61: ## 2. The actual 2G Cartier specialization
62: 
63: The exact doubling formula is
64: \[
65:  X(2G)=\frac{T^2}{4v^2}-(1-2v),\qquad
66:  Y(2G)=-v-\frac{T}{2v}X(2G).
67: \]
68: Consequently its lift has \(z=-2v/T\). In the small-resolution chart,
69: the equation \(zT+2v=0\) factors as
70: \[
71:  zT+2v
72:    =B\bigl(1-Bz^3-(1-2v)z^2\bigr).
73: \]
74: The B=0 factor is the extraneous -G section, introduced when the
75: formula \(z=X/(Y+v)\) is cleared of denominators. The actual 2G
76: closure has the Cartier equation
77: \[
78:                   1-Bz^3-(1-2v)z^2=0.
79: \]
80: It restricts at v=0 to \(1-z^2-Dz^4=0\), with multiplicity one,
81: and it does not contain C1. It misses G because at z=0 its defining
82: function is 1. Away from T=0, the displayed doubling formula tends
83: to O. Therefore the relative divisor \(2G-O\) specializes to C0
84: on this local source.
85: 
86: This also gives the global specialization on the compact minimal
87: source. The III* component degrees of \(2G-O\) are zero on every
88: nearby source and remain zero on its unchanged simultaneous root
89: charts. Any extra vertical divisor there is an integral whole
90: fibre, by the component intersection kernel. At the other finite
91: fibres the section tends to O and no component is added. Finally
92: \((2G-O)\cdot O=1=C_0\cdot O\), so the possible additional whole
93: fibre coefficient is zero. Thus the actual line for (a,b)=(2,1)
94: has limit \(O(C_0)\). Its class is retained even though the generic
95: Picard point 2G tends to zero.
96: 
97: For (a,b)=(1,2), G itself extends as the marked section above, so
98: the actual central line is \(O(G_0-O)\); it has component degrees
99: (-1,+1) on (C0,C1), and its generic point is nonzero two-torsion.
100: 
101: ## 3. Exact global Hom sections and their source-parameter winding
102: 
103: In the original normalized coordinates, put
104: \[
105:  f_{21}=\frac{y+1+(t/2)x}{x},\qquad
106:  f_{12}=\frac{y-1-(t/2)x}{x}.
107: \]
108: For (a,b)=(2,1), the numerator is the tangent line at -G;
109: for (a,b)=(1,2), it is the tangent line at +G. The denominator
110: is the vertical chord through G and -G. Their horizontal divisors
111: are exactly
112: \[
113:  D_{21}=(2G)-O-G+(-G),\qquad
114:  D_{12}=G-O-(-G)+(-2G).
115: \]
116: These are the actual lines of target-point linearizations for the
117: forward suspension by -bG. At III* the component contributions
118: cancel, because G and -G have the same nonidentity component and
119: 2G,-2G,O have the identity component. At finite fibres the displayed
120: functions have no whole-fibre zero or pole. Their vertical divisor
121: is therefore a multiple of the infinity fibre. Intersecting with O,
122: using the explicit polynomial coordinates of 2G, gives
123: \[
124:                 \operatorname{div}(f_{ab})=D_{ab}-F_\infty.
125: \]
126: Hence in the canonical meromorphic divisor frame the actual
127: linearization with selected simple whole zero is
128: \[
129:                        \phi_{ab}=\frac{t-t_0}{f_{ab}}.
130: \]
131: This fixes a concrete normalization of its scalar parameter.
132: 
133: Under \(X=u^2x,Y=u^3y,T=u^4t,v=u^3\), define
134: \[
135:  F_{21}=\frac{Y+v+(T/(2v))X}{X},\qquad
136:  F_{12}=\frac{Y-v-(T/(2v))X}{X}.
137: \]
138: Literal substitution gives \(f_{ab}=u^{-1}F_{ab}\). Consequently
139: \[
140:                       \phi_{ab}=\frac{q}{vF_{ab}}.              \tag{SZ.1}
141: \]
142: This is invariant under the marked mu3 coordinate quotient because v
143: is invariant. At the selected node \(X=v,Y=0,T=T_0\),
144: \[
145:  F_{21}=v/2,\qquad F_{12}=-v/2.
146: \]
147: In the local canonical line frame the Hom factor divided by q is
148: therefore exactly \(+2/v^2\), respectively \(-2/v^2\). It winds
149: twice negatively on a positive source-v loop.
150: 
151: The actual selected nodal Tate point of G is SM.7's
152: \[
153:  P_G=\frac{1+\sqrt{1+v}}{1-\sqrt{1+v}},\qquad vP_G\longrightarrow-4.
154: \]
155: The Tate base factor satisfies
156: \[
157:  q_T/q=-\frac{v}{64(1+v)^3}+O(q).
158: \]
159: Thus the three recorded source-parameter windings are respectively
160: qT/q:+1, PG:-1, and Hom/q:-2. In a sufficiently small selected collar,
161: the theta ratio identifying the actual Cartier line has a nonzero
162: limit 1 at the chosen nodal branch, so it does not change the latter
163: Hom-unit winding. This last branch comparison must also be retained
164: on the other node branch when the completion is glued.
165: 
